  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 209 AM CST Mon Nov 9 2026\n\nCurrent synoptic features show a relatively zonal jet stream pattern \nto our north, which has been the reason why we will continue to see \nour quiet weather regime for Monday and Tuesday. Due to the \ntemperature conditions we saw tonight, along with light winds, fog \nhas formed in parts of the state and around the Missouri River. This \nwill dissipate as the sun rises and temperatures increase. As we \nremain in this quiet pattern, dry conditions will prevail, along \nwith temperatures in the upper 60s and low 70s, which is 10-15 \ndegrees above average. \n\nThe pattern changes on Wednesday as the jet stream to our north \nforms a troughing feature across the eastern half of the CONUS, and \na ridging feature on the western half. The trough axis further \ndeveloping to our east on Wednesday will result in northerly flow at \nthe 500mb level. This will allow for our current air mass to \ninteract with colder Canadian air to our north, which will promote \nthe development of a low-pressure center. The feature will develop \nacross Iowa, and progress into Illinois by the afternoon hours on \nWednesday. Impacts include gusty winds from the northwest, isolated \nlight rain for the eastern half of Missouri, and lower temperatures \nmore November-like for the rest of the workweek. As the trough \nprogresses to our east, and the ridge moves into our area by next \nweekend, southerly flow will allow for a return for unseasonably \nwarm temperatures by next weekend.",
